# DTF tier manifest: azure-proxy-cache  (artifact-keeper#4320, 1.12.0 lane G7)
# Profile-set: storage.azurite (Azure Blob through Azurite as the DEFAULT
# backend) + storage.azurite-proxy-cache (AZURE_PROXY_CACHE_ENABLED=true, new)
# + upstreams.mock-static (a counting static upstream). Azurite is listed from
# the host with the azure-default-backend fixture azurite_blob.py.
#
# THE GAP
# #4320  An Azure deployment had no proxy cache on Azure: since #3923 it kept
#        the cache on the local filesystem under STORAGE_PATH, created remote
#        repositories on `filesystem`, refused an explicit `azure` remote with
#        400, and a multi-replica deployment needed STORAGE_PATH on shared
#        storage. The storage facade had no Azure arm because the Azure
#        backend could not list blobs.
#
# THE GATES
#   (FIX)     RED on ak-backend:1.11.5, GREEN on the lane image:
#             a remote created without storage_backend lands on `azure`; an
#             explicit `azure` remote is created (201); a proxied GET commits
#             its cache entry as Azure blobs (content and sidecar) and nothing
#             under /data/storage/proxy-cache, and the second GET is a cache hit;
#             POST cache/invalidate deletes the blobs and the next GET refetches.
#   (CONTROL) both images: the remote serves the upstream bytes, twice.
#
# Note: on the lane image the azure-default-backend tier's #3923 assertions
# (remote on `filesystem`, explicit `azure` refused) still hold, because the
# Azure proxy cache is opt-in and that tier does not opt in.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false; UPSTREAM_ALLOW_PRIVATE_IPS=true: the mock upstream
# is on a private subnet.
PROFILES="storage.azurite storage.azurite-proxy-cache upstreams.mock-static"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
UPSTREAM_ALLOW_PRIVATE_IPS="true"
